{
  "program": "G",
  "title": "A grammar rejection gate trades length fit for fewer wheel exceptions",
  "hard": false,
  "on_path": false,
  "statement": "With a 10% acceptance probability for out-of-grammar candidate words, median exception mass falls from .381 to .057 in ZL and .381 to .058 in IT; the real evaluation fractions are .051 and .047. Median token-length total variation rises from .052 to .104 and .042 to .092, respectively. Length-matched one-edit rates increase from .0247 to .0287 and .0214 to .0262, still below the corresponding real matched values of .0446 and .0433 for the filtered draws. Median candidate attempts per emitted token are about 1.51. Ten seeded draws quantify simulation variation, not uncertainty over manuscript pages.",
  "check": "Consider a fixed, compact rejection rule that does not require a full manuscript-fitted grammar; fit only on training pages and independently evaluate length distributions, rare support, adjacency and computational or description-length costs. Use a folio-clustered uncertainty analysis and held-out evaluation only after a reviewed scorer primitive exists.",
  "method": "Use the unchanged banks, ZL-training fitted length weights clipped to [0.25,4], and 10% previous-fragment sharing from P-G-032. After selecting a bank, weighted triple and optional shared fragment, classify the complete candidate with Stolfi NormalWord. In the filtered model accept a normal candidate unconditionally and an abnormal candidate with probability 0.1, otherwise redraw bank, triple, and optional sharing from scratch using the same preceding accepted token. The baseline emits all proposals. Reset adjacency at every valid sequence. Both models are run at 10 seeds 41001–41010, with lengths and sequence segmentation from each public evaluation transliteration. The 0.1 gate was chosen before these runs to approximately reduce .38 abnormal mass to .06; it directly uses a grammar derived from the manuscript and is an explicitly added global rule, not a blind discovery.",
  "metrics": "Measure token and observed-type EVA-basic length total variation, abnormal mass, repeated and exact one-edit adjacent rates. Following P-G-032, sample synthetic adjacent pairs conditional on the real adjacent pair length stratum, omitting unsupported strata, and compare to the retained real pairs and independent real samples of the same lengths. Report medians and min/max across simulation seeds as well as every draw.",
  "reproduction": "Extract the registry item P-G-024 to /tmp/p-g-024.json; obtain the Stolfi grammar from https://www.ic.unicamp.br/~stolfi/EXPORT/voynich/00-06-07-word-grammar/txt.n.html at /tmp/stolfi-grammar.html. Run the embedded script from the repository root with PYTHONPATH=. and pinned corpus files available. Input and script SHA-256 values are recorded below.",
  "input_sha256": {
    "registry_item": "df21ed0d0b628ee0a12e60b38a007830b8ce51c632b3643e14e45729d5f46400",
    "grammar_html": "a74a5224544d50f131f5b133023c835ad971dfef4098d4aa883026c7fb42bd55",
    "script": "2cce4574c250d9577ee055689b66a2b780026c860517207177b83c1abdf3d142"
  },
  "limitations": "The gate uses a complex hand-specified grammar and rejection, so it is not an independently spinning physical-wheel mechanism. The shared-fragment rule may modify length and changes acceptance rates; a model-complexity budget and page-bootstrap uncertainty remain open. Fixed training-fitted inventories may miss rare true forms, and these practice results are not a held-out verdict.",
